        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 822,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 676,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Rounds every value of a tensor DOWN to the whole number at or below it - down the number line, so 2.7 becomes 2 and -2.3 becomes -3.\n\nUse it to find which whole-numbered cell, bin or step a value falls in:\nfloor of a position divided by a cell size is the cell's index, and that stays right for negative positions too. Each element is rounded on its own, so the output has the shape of the input.\n\nFLOOR, CEIL AND TRUNC DIFFER FOR NEGATIVES:\nFor positive values floor and trunc agree. For negative values floor still goes DOWN (-2.3 becomes -3) while trunc goes towards zero (-2.3 becomes -2). If a value on the negative side lands one step lower than you expected, that is floor doing its job - use t.trunc if you meant \"drop the fraction\".\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.floor\nIt takes no arguments and has no parameters.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. A list, a NumPy array or a single number is turned into a float32 tensor first. Every input is processed and sent - this is the only inlet that makes the node send. An empty tensor sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe floored tensor, the same shape and type as the input - still floats, just with whole-number values. An integer tensor comes back unchanged.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe ramp climbs straight from -2.5 to 2.5; t.floor turns it into a staircase that always sits ON or UNDER the ramp: each step starts where the ramp crosses a whole number and stays flat until the next one. The message shows values either side of zero going down.\n\nRELATED:\nt.ceil goes up instead, t.trunc towards zero, t.round to the nearest. t.frac keeps the fractional part. floor does the same for numbers and NumPy arrays (np.floor shares its page).",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 660,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Rounds every value of a tensor DOWN to the whole number at or below it - down the number line, so 2.7 becomes 2 and -2.3 becomes -3.\n\nUse it to find which whole-numbered cell, bin or step a value falls in:\nfloor of a position divided by a cell size is the cell's index, and that stays right for negative positions too. Each element is rounded on its own, so the output has the shape of the input.\n\nFLOOR, CEIL AND TRUNC DIFFER FOR NEGATIVES:\nFor positive values floor and trunc agree. For negative values floor still goes DOWN (-2.3 becomes -3) while trunc goes towards zero (-2.3 becomes -2). If a value on the negative side lands one step lower than you expected, that is floor doing its job - use t.trunc if you meant \"drop the fraction\".\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.floor\nIt takes no arguments and has no parameters.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. A list, a NumPy array or a single number is turned into a float32 tensor first. Every input is processed and sent - this is the only inlet that makes the node send. An empty tensor sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe floored tensor, the same shape and type as the input - still floats, just with whole-number values. An integer tensor comes back unchanged.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe ramp climbs straight from -2.5 to 2.5; t.floor turns it into a staircase that always sits ON or UNDER the ramp: each step starts where the ramp crosses a whole number and stays flat until the next one. The message shows values either side of zero going down.\n\nRELATED:\nt.ceil goes up instead, t.trunc towards zero, t.round to the nearest. t.frac keeps the fractional part. floor does the same for numbers and NumPy arrays (np.floor shares its page)."
        },
